  <h2>Research interests</h2>
  <p>Applied econometrics for spatial and high-dimensional data, economic networks, international trade, and policy evaluation. Secondary interests include labor and AI, regional and environmental economics, remote sensing, and optimal transport.</p>

  <h2>Education</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>PhD, Economics</strong>, Georgia Institute of Technology, 2024. Minor in Quantitative Psychology. Dissertation on network centrality, spatial heterogeneity, and optimal transport in international trade.</li>
    <li><strong>MS, Geographic Information Science & Technology</strong>, Georgia Institute of Technology, 2022.</li>
    <li><strong>MS, Economics</strong>, Georgia Institute of Technology, 2019.</li>
    <li><strong>MA, Economics</strong>, Indiana University Bloomington, 2018.</li>
    <li><strong>MSc, Economics & Public Policy</strong>, Barcelona School of Economics, 2015.</li>
    <li><strong>BA, Economics</strong>, University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill, 2014.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Selected research</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>AI and the Entry-Level Labor Market</strong> (with Shane Vardanyan), working paper, 2026. Continuous-exposure event study and triple-difference analysis of generative AI and early-career U.S. employment using CPS microdata.</li>
    <li><strong>Trade in the Spotlight</strong> (with E. Gonchar), SSRN 5202676, 2025. Effective-distance panel framework for bilateral flows under visibility shocks.</li>
    <li><strong>The Rural Mobilization Gap</strong>, working paper. Community Development Entity fixed-effects decomposition of the New Markets Tax Credit.</li>
    <li><strong>Metric Sovereignty</strong>, living draft. Arrow's impossibility, AI governance, and capitalist democracy.</li>
    <li><strong>Penumbra</strong>, in progress. Topology and centrality in trade networks.</li>
    <li><strong>Asymmetric Returns on Exposure</strong>, theory paper in progress. Networked trade with heterogeneous firm exposure.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><a href="/research">Research pages, abstracts, and materials →</a></p>

  <h2>Teaching</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Instructor of record, Georgia Institute of Technology</strong>, three semesters in economics and policy and principles of microeconomics.</li>
    <li><strong>Graduate teaching assistant, Georgia Institute of Technology</strong>, undergraduate economics, graduate econometrics, and engineering signal processing.</li>
    <li><strong>Online and independent teaching</strong>, roughly 1,200 hours in a recent teaching year and 450+ students across test preparation, economics, statistics, econometrics, finance, and research methods.</li>
    <li><strong>Graduate Teaching Assistant of the Year</strong>, Georgia Institute of Technology, 2023.</li>
    <li>Creator of Stats Lab, Inference Lab, Macro Prep, Econ 101A, PPD 504 Studio, and Pictures of Inference.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><a href="/teaching">Teaching record and open learning tools →</a></p>

  <h2>Applied and advisory work</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Independent economic advisory, 2026–present</strong>, industrial-sector diagnostic methodology, indicator selection, composite-index construction, and international benchmarking design.</li>
    <li><strong>Quantitative research practice, 2024–present</strong>, applied econometric and measurement work for institutional and private clients, including support for student-led empirical projects.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Computational work</h2>
  <p>Python, R, Stata, Julia, and Go; spatial and remote-sensing workflows; LaTeX and Quarto; economic-data pipelines; interactive data products; entity and network analysis; and open-source teaching infrastructure.</p>
